POSNETEK_A = REC(
    "A theatre costume maker",
    "You will listen to an interview with Rosa Kent, who makes costumes for theatre and opera.",
    """Interviewer: Rosa, you've made costumes for some of the biggest theatres in Britain. How did you get into this job?

Rosa: Through my grandmother, really. She was a dressmaker, and when I was little I spent every Saturday in her workroom, playing with buttons and scraps of fabric. She gave me my first sewing machine when I was nine. By the time I was a teenager, I was making clothes for all my friends – some of them quite strange!

Interviewer: Did you study costume design?

Rosa: Not at first. My parents wanted me to have a "proper" career, so I studied history at university. But I spent all my free time working on student theatre productions, making costumes for nothing. After I graduated, I wrote to every theatre in London asking for work. Only one replied – they needed someone to wash and iron the costumes. I said yes immediately.

Interviewer: That's not very glamorous.

Rosa: Not at all! But I learned an enormous amount just by looking at how the costumes were made. After a year, the head of the costume department asked me to help with a small repair, and then with a whole dress. That was fifteen years ago.

Interviewer: What does a costume maker actually do?

Rosa: People think we just sew, but that's only part of it. First, the designer gives us drawings. Then we have to work out how to turn a drawing into something real that an actor can move, dance and sometimes fight in, eight times a week. My history degree is actually very useful, because for historical plays we research what people really wore – how a dress was fastened in 1750, for example.

Interviewer: Is there a costume you're particularly proud of?

Rosa: A dress for an opera singer playing a queen. It had a train six metres long and was covered in about three thousand hand-sewn glass beads. It took four of us two months to make. The problem was that it was so heavy that the singer could hardly breathe, and an opera singer needs to breathe! So we had to remake the whole top part with lighter materials. She sent us flowers on the first night.

Interviewer: What are the biggest challenges in your work?

Rosa: Time, always. Designers change their minds, actors change shape, and directors suddenly decide that a character needs to fall into a swimming pool. Once, two days before a premiere, we had to make a second copy of every costume because they were going to get wet in every performance.

Interviewer: Does anything go wrong during performances?

Rosa: All the time! Zips break, buttons fly off, a skirt gets caught on the scenery. There's always a costume person backstage with a needle and thread during every show. Quick changes are the most stressful – sometimes an actor has only thirty seconds to change completely.

Interviewer: What advice would you give to young people who want to do your job?

Rosa: Learn to sew really well – by hand as well as by machine. Go and see as much theatre as you can. And don't be too proud to start at the bottom. I started with a washing machine and an iron.""")

POSNETEK_B = REC(
    "The history of the potato",
    "You will listen to a conversation with food historian Dr Lucy Barnes and potato farmer Peter Walsh.",
    """Presenter: Today we're talking about one of the most important foods in the world – the humble potato. Lucy, where does the potato come from?

Lucy: From the Andes mountains in South America, in what is now Peru and Bolivia. People there started growing potatoes around seven thousand years ago, and they developed thousands of different varieties. In Peru today there are still around four thousand – in all sorts of colours, including purple, red and even black.

Presenter: How did potatoes reach Europe?

Lucy: The Spanish brought them back in the second half of the sixteenth century, after they conquered South America. But Europeans didn't like them at first. Potatoes grow underground, they aren't mentioned in the Bible, and some people believed that they caused diseases. For a long time they were used mainly to feed animals.

Presenter: So what changed?

Lucy: Mostly hunger. Potatoes grow well in poor soil and in cold climates, and they produce far more food per field than wheat. Some rulers realised this and tried to persuade people to grow them. In Prussia, King Frederick the Great ordered farmers to plant potatoes. In France, a scientist called Parmentier is said to have planted a field of potatoes near Paris and put soldiers to guard it during the day. People thought that anything guarded so carefully must be valuable, so they stole the potatoes at night – which is exactly what he wanted.

Presenter: That's a wonderful story.

Lucy: It may not be completely true, but it shows how hard it was to change people's habits.

Presenter: Peter, you grow potatoes in Ireland. Of course, Ireland has a tragic history with potatoes.

Peter: It does. By the 1840s, about a third of the Irish population depended almost completely on potatoes. Then, in 1845, a disease called blight arrived, and the potatoes rotted in the ground. During the famine that followed, about a million people died and another million emigrated, many of them to America. My own great-great-grandfather left for Boston and never came back.

Presenter: Is blight still a problem today?

Peter: Very much so. It's the biggest worry for every potato farmer. In a wet summer, it can destroy a field in a week. We check our plants every day, and scientists are trying to develop varieties that can resist it. Interestingly, some of the old varieties from the Andes are naturally resistant, so the answer may come from where the potato began.

Presenter: How many potatoes do you grow?

Peter: About two thousand tonnes a year, mostly for supermarkets and for making crisps. Some of my varieties are best for chips, others for boiling. People don't realise how different they are.

Presenter: Lucy, is the potato still important today?

Lucy: Hugely. It's one of the most important food crops in the world, after rice, wheat and maize. And it even went to space – in 1995, potatoes became the first vegetable to be grown on a space shuttle.

Peter: Not bad for something that people once thought was only good enough for pigs.""")

